---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "introduction", label: "Introduction", heading: false },
  { id: "initialization", label: "Initialization" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "markup-options", label: "Options in markup" },
  { id: "watch", label: "Markup added later" },
  { id: "components", label: "Components" },
  { id: "ignoring", label: "Ignoring elements" },
  { id: "events", label: "Events" },
  { id: "alpine", label: "Alpine.js" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="auto_init" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.introduction}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Auto Init starts all of the registered Expressive components with a single call.
          The IIFE bundle exposes it as <code>Expressive.AutoInit</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Call <code>AutoInit()</code> yourself after the DOM is ready. Importing the JavaScript
          installs a few document-level behaviors (Forms, Chips, Slider, Cards, ExpandingCard,
          Dialogs, BottomSheets, and SideSheets), but it does
          <strong>not</strong> call <code>AutoInit()</code> for you.
          This documentation site calls it in <code>docs.js</code> on <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.initialization}>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  Expressive.AutoInit();
});
`} />
        <p>Or pass a root element and per-component options. The option keys are the component names from the table below.</p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.AutoInit(document.body, {
  Menu: {
    // pass options for Menu here
  },
  FloatingActionButton: {
    // pass options for FloatingActionButton here
  }
});
`} />
        <p>From the module build:</p>
<Code code={`
import { AutoInit, Tooltip } from './js/expressive.mjs';

AutoInit();

const element = document.querySelector('.custom-tooltip');
Tooltip.init(element, { position: 'top' });
`} />
        <p>Component <code>init()</code> methods also accept a <code>NodeList</code> or <code>HTMLCollection</code> and return an array of instances. An empty collection returns an empty array.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.options}>
        <p><code>AutoInit(context, options, settings)</code> takes three arguments:</p>
        <OptionsTable>
            <tr>
              <td><code>context</code></td>
              <td>Element</td>
              <td><code>document.body</code></td>
              <td>DOM element or shadow root to search within for components.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>options</code></td>
              <td>Object</td>
              <td><code>{"{}"}</code></td>
              <td>Options for each component, keyed by name. See that component's page for its own options.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>settings</code></td>
              <td>Object</td>
              <td><code>{"{}"}</code></td>
              <td><code>{"{ watch: true }"}</code> keeps starting components in markup added to <code>context</code> later. See <a href="#watch">Markup added later</a>.</td>
            </tr>
        </OptionsTable>
        <p>
          You can call <code>AutoInit()</code> again on the same elements. Each component's constructor
          destroys any existing instance on that element before creating a new one.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S["markup-options"]}>
        <p>
          To give an element its own options for <code>AutoInit()</code>, add a
          <code>data-<em>name</em>-options</code> attribute that holds a JSON object. The name is
          the component's name from the table below in kebab case. The keys are its option
          names as written in script.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<a class="button menu-trigger" data-target="actions"
   data-menu-options='{"closeOnClick": false, "placement": "top"}'>Actions</a>

<a class="button tooltipped" data-tooltip="Saved"
   data-tooltip-options='{"enterDelay": 300}'>Save</a>

<select data-form-select-options='{"classes": "wide"}'>...</select>
`} />
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code>Menu</code> reads <code>data-menu-options</code>, <code>FormSelect</code> reads
            <code>data-form-select-options</code>, and <code>FloatingActionButton</code> reads
            <code>data-floating-action-button-options</code>. The name comes from the table, not the
            class, so a <code>.date-picker</code> reads <code>data-datepicker-options</code>. An element that is both a tooltip and a
            menu trigger reads each attribute for its own component.
          </li>
          <li>
            Options passed to <code>AutoInit(context, options)</code> win for the keys they set. A
            nested object such as <code>i18n</code> counts as one key. Tooltip reads its own
            <code>data-tooltip</code>, <code>data-tooltip-id</code> and <code>data-position</code>
            again each time it opens, so those win over both. Set the position, and Menu's
            <code>contextMenu</code> (<code>data-context-menu</code>), in one place.
          </li>
          <li>
            Markup can set an option whose default is a string, number, boolean, array or plain
            object, with a value of the same kind. <code>AutoInit()</code> checks keys inside an object
            such as <code>i18n</code> the same way. If it rejects one, the whole object keeps its default. An
            object whose default is empty, such as FormSelect's <code>menuOptions</code>, accepts no
            keys from markup. When the default is a non-empty array, the items need the kind
            of its first item. Callbacks, elements and options whose default is <code>null</code>
            cannot come from markup; pass those from script. Autocomplete's
            <code>allowUnsafeHTML</code> and the <code>data</code> lists of Chips and Autocomplete
            are script-only too.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>AutoInit()</code> treats the attribute as untrusted. It skips invalid JSON, an unknown
            key or a value of the wrong kind with a console warning, and that option keeps its default. A value that
            passes these checks but still makes a component throw stops <code>AutoInit()</code>, as
            the same value passed from script would.
          </li>
          <li>
            Only <code>AutoInit()</code> reads the attribute. A direct <code>init()</code> call uses
            the options it is given. Menus inside a <code>.menubar</code> take their options from
            Menubar's <code>menuOptions</code>, so <code>data-menu-options</code> has no effect on
            them.
          </li>
        </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.watch}>
        <p>
          <code>AutoInit()</code> scans <code>context</code> once. To keep watching it, pass
          <code>{"{ watch: true }"}</code> as the third argument. Components then start in markup added
          later, and <code>AutoInit()</code> destroys them when their markup is removed. The call returns
          a function that stops watching.
        </p>
<Code code={`
const stop = Expressive.AutoInit(document.body, {}, { watch: true });

// Starts a Menu once the observer sees the trigger, without calling init.
document.querySelector('main').insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', \`
  <a class="button menu-trigger" data-target="more">More</a>
  <menu id="more"><li><a href="#!">Share</a></li></menu>\`);

// In your view's teardown. Components already started keep running.
function unmount() {
  stop();
}
`} />
        <ul>
          <li>
            Added markup starts the same way the first scan does, with the same <code>options</code>,
            <code>data-<em>name</em>-options</code> attributes and <code>no-autoinit</code> opt-out. The
            added element is checked as well as its descendants. Components start in a later microtask,
            when the browser reports the change, not during the call that adds the markup.
          </li>
          <li>
            An element that already has an instance keeps it. Starting a component yourself before
            adding its element works, and components that build their own markup, such as the
            Datepicker's month and year selects, keep the instances they made.
          </li>
          <li>
            Removed markup destroys the instances <code>AutoInit()</code> would have started. Elements
            with <code>no-autoinit</code> are left to your code. An element moved elsewhere in the
            page keeps its instance. One that is removed and added back later starts again.
          </li>
          <li>
            Only added and removed elements count. Adding <code>tooltipped</code> to an element already in the page does not start a Tooltip; call <code>init</code> for that.
          </li>
          <li>
            Markup inserted at runtime becomes live components. If you insert user content, sanitize
            it first and drop component classes and <code>data-<em>name</em>-options</code> attributes
            you don't want started.
          </li>
          <li>
            Watch mode does not enter shadow roots. To watch markup inside one, call
            <code>AutoInit(shadowRoot, options, {"{ watch: true }"})</code> for that root.
          </li>
          <li>
            Call it once per root. A component that throws while starting stops the rest of that
            batch, the same way it stops <code>AutoInit()</code>. The watcher still handles later changes.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <code>stop()</code> first handles changes the browser has not reported yet, so markup added
          just before the call still starts. Without <code>watch</code>, <code>AutoInit()</code>
          returns a function that does nothing.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.components}>
        <p>
          These are the components <code>AutoInit()</code> starts, and the selector each one claims.
          Elements that also have <code>no-autoinit</code> are skipped.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Selector</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>AppBar</code></td><td><code>header.medium, header.large, header:has(&gt; :is(nav:not(.tabs, .navigation-bar, .navigation-rail), .bar) &gt; .search-bar)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Autocomplete</code></td><td><code>.autocomplete</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ButtonGroup</code></td><td><code>.button-group</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cards</code></td><td><code>article:has(&gt; aside[id]:not([id=""])):not(:has(&gt; aside ~ aside)):has(&gt; button.card-reveal-trigger[type="button"], &gt; :not(aside) button.card-reveal-trigger[type="button"])</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ExpandingCard</code></td><td><code>article.expanding-card:has(&gt; dialog.expanding-card-dialog)</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Carousel</code></td><td><code>.carousel</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Chips</code></td><td><code>.chips</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Confetti</code></td><td><code>.confetti</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Particles</code></td><td><code>canvas.particles</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Keypad</code></td><td><code>.keypad, .keyboard</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ColorPicker</code></td><td><code>.color-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>BarChart</code></td><td><code>.bar-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PieChart</code></td><td><code>.pie-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>HeatmapChart</code></td><td><code>.heatmap-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>RadarChart</code></td><td><code>.radar-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>RadialChart</code></td><td><code>.radial-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>MixedChart</code></td><td><code>.mixed-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ScatterChart</code></td><td><code>.scatter-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ColumnChart</code></td><td><code>.column-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CommandPalette</code></td><td><code>dialog.command-palette</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Questionnaire</code></td><td><code>form.questionnaire</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Datepicker</code></td><td><code>.date-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>LineChart</code></td><td><code>.line-chart</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Menu</code></td><td><code>.menu-trigger</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>MessageScroller</code></td><td><code>.message-scroller</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>PullToRefresh</code></td><td><code>.pull-to-refresh</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Lightbox</code></td><td><code>.lightboxed</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>NavigationRail</code></td><td><code>.navigation-rail</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ScrollSpy</code></td><td><code>.scrollspy</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>FormSelect</code></td><td><code>select</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Tabs</code></td><td><code>.tabs</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Timepicker</code></td><td><code>.time-picker</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Tooltip</code></td><td><code>.tooltipped</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>FloatingActionButton</code></td><td><code>.fab-menu</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Menubar</code></td><td><code>.menubar</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Snackbar, CharacterCounter, and Slider stay out of this table. Slider still starts itself when the
          bundle loads. Forms, Chips, Cards, ExpandingCard, Dialogs, BottomSheets, and SideSheets also run an
          import-time <code>Init()</code>. Chips, Cards, and ExpandingCard appear in the table as well so a
          later <code>AutoInit()</code> can pick up elements added after load.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.ignoring}>
        <p>
          To make <code>AutoInit()</code> skip an element, add the class
          <code>no-autoinit</code>. Initialize that element yourself with the component's
          <code>init</code> method when you need options other than the defaults.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<a class="button tooltipped no-autoinit" data-tooltip="Hi">Hover</a>

Expressive.Tooltip.init(
  document.querySelector('.tooltipped.no-autoinit'),
  { position: 'top' }
);
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.events}>
        <p>
          Menu, Lightbox, NavigationRail, Cards and ExpandingCard fire DOM events from the
          element they were initialized on. <code>expressive:open</code> and
          <code>expressive:close</code> fire when the state changes, and
          <code>expressive:opened</code> and <code>expressive:closed</code> when the motion
          ends. Cards and ExpandingCard fire only <code>expressive:open</code> and
          <code>expressive:close</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each event comes right after the matching <code>on…</code> callback, which still
          works. The events bubble and cross shadow roots, so one listener on the document
          sees every component. Events from nested components bubble too, so check <code>event.target</code>.
          Closing a Menu or Lightbox from its <code>expressive:open</code> listener stops it from opening.
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('expressive:open', (event) => {
  if (event.target.matches('.menu-trigger')) analytics.track('menu-open');
});
`} />
        <p>Choices and changes carry what was chosen in <code>event.detail</code>:</p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="striped">
          <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Fired by</th><th><code>detail</code></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:change</code></td><td>Tabs, Carousel</td><td><code>{"{ panel }"}</code>, <code>{"{ item, dragged }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:select</code></td><td>Menu, Datepicker, Timepicker</td><td><code>{"{ item }"}</code>, <code>{"{ date }"}</code>, <code>{"{ hours, minutes }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:confirm</code>, <code>expressive:cancel</code></td><td>Datepicker, Timepicker</td><td>none</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:autocomplete</code></td><td>Autocomplete</td><td><code>{"{ values }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:create</code></td><td>Autocomplete, from its "Add" row</td><td><code>{"{ text }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:chip-add</code>, <code>chip-select</code>, <code>chip-delete</code></td><td>Chips</td><td><code>{"{ chip }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:change</code></td><td>ButtonGroup, Menu checkbox and radio items, ScrollSpy (from the section), Tour</td><td><code>{"{ button, pressed }"}</code>, <code>{"{ item, checked }"}</code>, <code>{"{ link }"}</code>, <code>{"{ step, index }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:open</code>, <code>expressive:close</code></td><td>Tooltip, Snackbar, Tour</td><td>Tour's close: <code>{"{ completed }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:action</code>, <code>expressive:closed</code></td><td>Snackbar</td><td>none</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:copy</code></td><td>Copy buttons</td><td><code>{"{ text, copied }"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>expressive:change</code>, <code>expressive:sort</code></td><td>Data tables, from the wrapper</td><td><code>{"{ selected }"}</code>, <code>{"{ header, column, direction }"}</code>; <code>sort</code> is cancelable</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          An Autocomplete or Select input hosts a Menu, so it also fires that Menu's
          <code>expressive:open</code>, <code>expressive:close</code> and
          <code>expressive:select</code>. A callback that changes the choice again, such as
          an <code>onShow</code> that selects another tab, leaves only the event for the
          final choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tooltip fires <code>expressive:open</code> and <code>expressive:close</code> when it
          shows and hides, after its delays. A snackbar fires its events from the
          snackbar itself. <code>expressive:closed</code> comes just before it leaves the page.
          Tour's <code>expressive:close</code> says whether the Done button finished it.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.alpine}>
        <p>
          <code>AutoInit()</code> scans the page once, so it misses anything Alpine renders later with
          <code>x-if</code> or <code>x-for</code>. <a href="#watch">Watch mode</a> starts and destroys that
          markup with the options of the <code>AutoInit()</code> call and its <code>data-<em>name</em>-options</code>
          attributes. To pass options from Alpine expressions instead, register a directive. Alpine runs it each time it
          creates the element and calls <code>cleanup</code> when it removes it, so every instance gets
          destroyed with its element.
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('alpine:init', () => {
  Alpine.directive('expressive', (el, { value, expression }, { evaluate, cleanup }) => {
    // HTML lowercases attribute names, so match the export name the same way.
    const name = Object.keys(Expressive).find((key) => key.toLowerCase() === value);
    const instance = Expressive[name].init(el, expression ? evaluate(expression) : {});
    cleanup(() => instance.destroy());
  });
});
`} />
        <p>
          Write the component name after the colon and pass options as the expression. Add
          <code>no-autoinit</code> so a later <code>AutoInit()</code> call leaves the element alone.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<a class="button tooltipped no-autoinit" data-tooltip="Hi"
   x-expressive:tooltip="{ position: 'top' }">Hover</a>
`} />
        <p>
          Select, Autocomplete and Datepicker fire a native <code>change</code> event when the user picks a
          value, so <code>x-model</code> picks it up. When your code sets the value, the visible field stays
          stale until you call <code>refresh()</code>. Do the same after adding or removing options.
          Put the select in its own <code>.field</code> so the template root stays the element Alpine created.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<template x-if="open">
  <div class="field">
    <select id="letter" class="no-autoinit" x-model="letter" x-expressive:formselect
      x-effect="letter; $nextTick(() => $el.Expressive_FormSelect?.refresh())">
      <option value="a">Alpha</option>
      <option value="b">Beta</option>
    </select>
    <label for="letter">Letter</label>
  </div>
</template>
`} />
        <p>
          Component events bubble, so Alpine listens to them like any other:
          <code>@expressive:change="tab = $event.detail.panel.id"</code>. Dialogs and sheets are native
          <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> elements. Open one with <code>$refs.dialog.showModal()</code>.
        </p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
